---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy & Temporary File Processing | compressimage.fun"
  description="Plain-language details about server processing, four-hour deletion, Delete now, browser-only Base64 tools, logs, and analytics."
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
>
  <header class="hero compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Plain-language privacy</div><h1>Your files are temporary</h1><p
        class="lead"
      >
        Server tools need the image to do the work. Browser-only tools do not upload it. Here is the
        exact boundary.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <article class="article shell">
    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="article-summary">
        Files processed on compressimage.fun servers are automatically deleted within four hours.
        You can delete a completed job immediately.
      </p>
      <section>
        <h2>Server tools</h2><p>
          Compression, resizing, cropping, format conversion, metadata tools, watermarks, and
          favicon generation upload images to the same-origin processing API. Each temporary job
          uses a cryptographically random ID and a separate high-entropy access token. Files are
          stored under random internal names, not your filename.
        </p><p>
          The source, outputs, small private previews, and safe job metadata live in an isolated
          processor volume. A cleanup task runs at startup and periodically. Delete now removes the
          complete job directory immediately.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Browser tools</h2><p>
          Image to Base64, Base64 to Image, Base64 Image Viewer, and Image to Data URI run in
          browser memory. Their file or pasted value is not sent to the processing API. The tools
          also set input limits to avoid freezing your tab.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Logs</h2><p>
          Operational logs may contain a request ID, tool ID, format, coarse size bucket, duration,
          and a controlled error category. They do not intentionally contain image bytes, Base64
          data, GPS values, access tokens, result URLs, or full original filenames.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Analytics</h2><p>
          Analytics is off when no measurement ID is configured. If enabled later, the event layer
          sends only controlled categories such as tool ID, format, file-count bucket, and
          processing result. Filenames, image contents, EXIF, job IDs, access tokens, and free-form
          errors are excluded.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>What metadata removal cannot do</h2><p>
          Removing EXIF and GPS fields does not hide information visible in the pixels. Faces,
          signs, reflections, and document text may still reveal private details.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
